<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zhangchu</h1><p class="st-meta">209 BCE – 208 BCE · capital Chen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived regime of Chen Sheng and Wu Guang, leaders of the first great peasant rising in Chinese history, which set the Qin empire ablaze.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 209 BCE a party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-guang">Wu Guang</a> was held up by rain at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dazexiang-uprising">Dazexiang</a> in Anhui. Facing execution for lateness under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> law, they rebelled, asking whether kings and nobles were born to their station. Chen Sheng took the city of Chen and proclaimed himself king of Zhangchu, 'Expanding Chu', reviving the name of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zhangchu was more a rebel movement than a state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> sent generals in all directions, and in the turmoil the kingdoms of Zhao, Wei, Qi and Yan were revived under their own kings. His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-wen">Zhou Wen</a> came within reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> capital before being driven back by the Qin commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-guang">Wu Guang</a> was murdered by his own officers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xingyang">Xingyang</a>, and late in 209 or early in 208 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> was killed by his charioteer, Zhuang Jia, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han's</a> armies advanced. The rising had shown the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> could be defeated, and other rebels, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, carried it on.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 209 BCE – 208 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.54%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>209 BCE – 208 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chen Sheng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhang Chu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-208&amp;focus=zhangchu" aria-label="Zhangchu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7442 1019 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zhangchu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7762 1275h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7762 1275h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-208&amp;focus=zhangchu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

